.TH "OSMIUM-FILE-FORMATS" "5" "1.19.1" "" ""
.SH NAME
osmium\-file\-formats \- OSM file formats known to Osmium
.SH FILE TYPES
OSM uses three types of files for its main data:
.TP
\f[B]Data files\f[R]
These are the most common files.
They contain the OSM data from a specific point in time.
This can either be a planet file containing \f[I]all\f[R] OSM data or
some kind of extract.
At most one version of every object (node, way, or relation) is
contained in this file.
Deleted objects are \f[I]not\f[R] in this file.
The usual suffix used is \f[CR].osm\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]History files\f[R]
These files contain not only the current version of an object, but their
history, too.
So for any object (node, way, or relation) there can be zero or more
versions in this file.
Deleted objects can also be in this file.
The usual suffix used is \f[CR].osm\f[R] or \f[CR].osh\f[R].
Because sometimes the same suffix is used as for normal data files
(\f[CR].osm\f[R]) and because there is no clear indicator in the header,
it is not always clear what type of file you have in front of you.
.TP
\f[B]Change files\f[R]
Sometimes called \f[I]diff files\f[R] or \f[I]replication diffs\f[R]
these files contain the changes between one state of the OSM database
and another state.
Change files can contains several versions of an object and also deleted
objects.
The usual suffix used is \f[CR].osc\f[R].
.PP
All these files have in common that they contain OSM objects (nodes,
ways, and relations).
History files and change files can contain several versions of the same
object and also deleted objects, data files can\(cqt.
.PP
Where possible, Osmium commands can handle all file types.
For some commands only some file types make sense.
.SH FORMATS
The \f[B]osmium\f[R] command line tool supports all major OSM file
formats plus some more.
These are:
.IP \(bu 2
The classical XML format in the variants \f[I].osm\f[R] (for data
files), \f[I].osh\f[R] (for data files with history) and \f[I].osc\f[R]
(for change files).
.IP \(bu 2
The PBF binary format (usually with suffix \f[I].osm.pbf\f[R] or just
\f[I].pbf\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
The OPL format (usually with suffix \f[I].osm.opl\f[R] or just
\f[I].opl\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
The O5M/O5C format (usually with suffix \f[I].o5m\f[R] or
\f[I].o5c\f[R]) (reading only).
.IP \(bu 2
The \(lqdebug\(rq format (usually with suffix \f[I].osm.debug\f[R])
(writing only).
.PP
In addition files in all formats except PBF can be compressed using
\f[I]gzip\f[R] or \f[I]bzip2\f[R].
(Add \f[I].gz\f[R] or \f[I].bz2\f[R] suffixes, respectively.)
.SH AUTODETECTION
Which format a file has is usually autodetected from the file name
suffix.
.PP
If this doesn\(cqt work, either because you are reading from STDIN or
writing to STDOUT, or because you have an unusual file name, you have to
set the format manually.
You can also set the format manually if you want to specify special
format options.
.PP
Most \f[B]osmium\f[R] commands support the
\f[B]\-\-input\-format/\-F\f[R] and \f[B]\-\-output\-format/\-f\f[R]
options to set the format.
They take a comma\-separated list of arguments, the first is the format,
further arguments set additional options.
.SH SPECIAL FORMAT OPTIONS
The following options can be added when writing OSM files:
.TP
\f[CR]history\f[R]=\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R]
Explicitly set whether this file allows multiple versions of the same
object or not.
By default set to \f[CR]true\f[R] for \f[I].osh\f[R] and \f[I].osc\f[R]
files, to \f[CR]false\f[R] otherwise.
.TP
\f[CR]xml_change_format\f[R]=\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R]
Enable/disable XML change format.
Same as \f[I].osc\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]force_visible_flag\f[R]=\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R] (\f[I]default: false\f[R])
Force writing of visible flag, even for normal OSM XML files.
.TP
\f[CR]pbf_dense_nodes\f[R]=\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R] (\f[I]default: true\f[R])
Enable/disable DenseNodes format for PBF files.
.TP
\f[CR]pbf_compression\f[R]=\f[CR]none\f[R]/\f[CR]zlib\f[R]/\f[CR]lz4\f[R] (\f[I]default: zlib\f[R])
Set compression type in PBF files.
\f[CR]zlib\f[R] (or \f[CR]true\f[R]) is the default and almost all files
use this.
\f[CR]none\f[R] (or \f[CR]false\f[R]) disables compression which will
make writing files a bit faster, but the resulting files are 2 to 3
times bigger.
The \f[CR]lz4\f[R] compression is not quite as good as \f[CR]zlib\f[R]
but much faster to compress and decompress, it is currently not
supported by most OSM file readers.
.TP
\f[CR]pbf_compression_level\f[R]=\&...
Set compression level for PBF.
Available values and default depend on the compression type used, see
the OSM File Formats Manual for details.
.TP
\f[CR]add_metadata\f[R]=\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R]/\&... (\f[I]default: true\f[R])
Enable/disable writing of object metadata such as changeset id,
username, etc.
Disabling this will make files a bit smaller.
This can also be set to other values, see the OSM File Formats Manual
for details.
.TP
\f[CR]locations_on_ways\f[R]=\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R] (\f[I]default: false\f[R])
Add node locations to way nodes.
(PBF, XML, OPL only.)
.TP
\f[CR]use_color\f[R]=\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R] (\f[I]default: false\f[R])
Output with ANSI colors.
(DEBUG format only.)
.TP
\f[CR]add_crc32\f[R]=\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R] (\f[I]default: false\f[R])
Add CRC32 checksum to all objects.
(DEBUG format only.)
.SH EXAMPLES
Here are some examples:
.TP
\f[CR]pbf\f[R]
PBF format.
.TP
\f[CR]pbf,add_metadata=false\f[R]
PBF format, don\(cqt write metadata
.TP
\f[CR]osm.bz2\f[R]
XML format, compressed with bzip2.
.TP
\f[CR]osc.gz\f[R]
OSM change file, compressed with gzip.
.TP
\f[CR]osm.gz,xml_change_format=true\f[R]
OSM change file, compressed with gzip.
.TP
\f[CR]osh.opl\f[R]
OSM history file in OPL format.
